=80=99s mother, played by Laura Ramoso, asks her daughter why she has such =
small breasts. (=E2=80=9CIt </span><span style=3D"font-style:italic">certai=
nly</span><span> does not run in the family.=E2=80=9D) She then volunteers =
her for a game of volleyball with strangers before expressing incredulity t=
hat Laura has cramps. (=E2=80=9CYou have cramps=3F I have </span><span styl=
e=3D"font-style:italic">never</span><span> had cramps in my life.=E2=80=9D)=
</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margi=
n:0 0 15px"><span>For my family, the unfiltered Germanic bluntness on displ=
ay in Ramoso=E2=80=99s clips feels like a private joke. To me, she=E2=80=99=
s a version of my mother. To my kids, she=E2=80=99s me. But her videos also=
 get millions of views, from people all over the world, and I wanted to und=
erstand why.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;colo=
r:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>We=E2=80=99re used to thinking that humor doe=
sn=E2=80=99t translate easily. It doesn=E2=80=99t travel well compared to, =
say, drama or music. Everyone gets a love story, even with subtitles. A jok=
e that relies on specific cultural codes and implicit references=3F That=E2=
=80=99s harder.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;c=
olor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But Ramoso and a growing number of comedia=
ns, with the help of the internet, are testing that premise. What if we=E2=
=80=99re in an age where humor increasingly </span><span style=3D"font-styl=
e:italic">does</span><span> translate=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal =
17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-wei=
ght:700">An ancient form</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgi=
a,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Ramoso had her breakthrough durin=
g Covid, when stage jobs dried up and she started posting shorts online. Th=
e first of her videos that went viral was a series called =E2=80=9CMy Germa=
n mother visiting me in Canada.=E2=80=9D After that she added more characte=
rs to the mix. Her =E2=80=9CThe Girl Who Just Got Back From (fill in the bl=
ank)=E2=80=9D series mocks the tourist whose personality changes after a sh=
ort trip overseas. She also does her Italian dad, as well as Chiara, the ma=
ddeningly unhelpful </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/a/A-HXDFUadb=
FPmOQyFQ_Z3A~~/AAAAARA~/6h9oGdPOHnqzGa9jVy3sF2DrIkAJqaSQqW6M9rxAfOHI_mEcE28=
s14oYz8uNZJrEXOhKwsB-hKk19I7QLzqH1bJYko46qgnwokSO8wjS9mVtkgXCZ9i8I7rHMBmmaL=
3LyLSVoZ1vp00PzOf2S4vkeXqSXZr5q9_YZBzaDjAj0yTTCzuHGsWa0odFJ4QTB2pLpj4AxxwJT=
eIu-vFUWWa0Iis-i6uJxwZSn0WLSOwcJtP3mv1_KCQ_Gf-g_5Uq7NqsdYstF7rE-5CMkzbrB8dT=
_UUKDRcpan7pJtVNWg9XkIdW26YniUCm2-1_j1_-TwxFrClF0Qqh6X_1ksjQdMK03A~~" class=
=3D"css-sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;te=
xt-decoration:underline">Italian check-in agent</a><span>. (=E2=80=9CYou wi=
ll miss your flight=3F Send the flight a letter: =E2=80=98I miss you, fligh=
t.=E2=80=99 =E2=80=9D)</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,=
serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Ramoso=E2=80=99s videos feature dif=
ferent caricatures, but they tend to follow the same format: Ramoso making =
rapid-fire jokes, underscored by quick cuts.</span></p><p style=3D"font:nor=
mal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It turns out =
that, in both form and content, Ramoso may have been tapping into features =
that help humor cross borders.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px =
georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I spoke to Giselinde Kuiper=
s, a Dutch sociologist who studies cross-cultural humor. Some humor has alw=
ays traveled better, she said. Slapstick, for example, which relies on phys=
icality, not language. Cultural stereotypes are interesting, she said, beca=
use almost every culture has them; they=E2=80=99re one of the most ancient =
forms of humor out there. (The oldest surviving joke book, Kuipers said, is=
 a collection from late antiquity called the Philogelos, or Laughter Lover,=
 in which Roman citizens mock provincial bumpkins.)</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Humor =
based on stereotypes may be universal as a form, but historically it hasn=
=E2=80=99t traveled well. Stereotypes need to be recognizable to be funny. =
But the internet and social media have had two effects, Kuipers said. In th=
e same way that American sitcoms have long been exportable because their fo=
rmat has trained the world on how to recognize something =E2=80=9Cfunny=E2=
=80=9D =E2=80=94 a contained setting, canned laughter =E2=80=94 the video s=
horts on TikTok and Instagram have their own visual grammar that signal =E2=
=80=9Cfunny=E2=80=9D: fast talk, jerky edits.</span></p><p style=3D"font:no=
rmal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>At the same =
time, these platforms have lowered cultural barriers even further, and made=
 stereotypes more recognizable. You no longer have to have your own German =
mum to watch Ramoso, as her mother, saying =E2=80=9CAch, Laura,=E2=80=9D an=
d =E2=80=9CYou have so many clothes and none of them are good,=E2=80=9D and=
 laugh.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#33=
