and tells them, =E2=80=9CBe kind and try your best.=E2=80=9D It=E2=80=99s b=
ecome a daily ritual, our attempt to remind them to be good people. Will it=
 work=3F Who knows! But lately, with all this talk about A.I., I=E2=80=99ve=
 begun to see our daily admonition a little differently.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>A=
bout 15 years ago, I was at a conference where a technologist raised the po=
ssibility of machines acting independently of humans. The notion blew my mi=
nd. Don=E2=80=99t humans program machines=3F I asked him, =E2=80=9CDon=E2=
=80=99t we have the power to make them what we want them to be=3F=E2=80=9D<=
/span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin=
:0 0 15px"><span>His explanation changed the way I thought about A.I.: Trai=
ning a machine-learning model is like parenting a child, he said. You can p=
ass your values on; you can teach your child how to behave with incentives =
and rewards. But you can=E2=80=99t control who your child ultimately turns =
out to be.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:=
#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That was a long time ago. We=E2=80=99ve learned=
 a lot about A.I. since then, though we=E2=80=99re still learning about wha=
t we can and can=E2=80=99t control. I=E2=80=99ve also learned a lot about p=
arenting. Training an A.I. is not like parenting a child.</span></p><p styl=
e=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=
But I still find myself thinking there=E2=80=99s something to the metaphor.=
 How do we make teenagers care about their parents, or the world=3F Call it=
